# What the terminal answers: DSR, CPR, DECXCPR, DA1, DA2, DA3, XTVERSION, DECRQM, DECRQSS,
# window reports and color queries. In a pattern (`reply~`) `*` stands for any run of bytes.
#
# Which terminal a DA reply names is the engine's choice, so those cases only check the form.

test DSR 5 reports that the terminal is ready
spec xterm ctlseqs CSI 5 n: the result "OK" is CSI 0 n
size 10 4
feed \e[5n
reply \e[0n

test DSR 6 reports the cursor position
spec xterm ctlseqs CSI 6 n: the result is CSI r ; c R
size 10 4
feed \e[6n
reply \e[1;1R
feed \e[3;7H\e[6n
reply \e[3;7R

test DSR 6 reports the absolute position when origin mode is off
spec VT100 User Guide CPR: the numbering of lines depends on origin mode
size 10 4
feed \e[2;3r\e[3;4H\e[6n
reply \e[3;4R

test DSR 6 is relative to the margins in origin mode
spec VT100 User Guide CPR: the numbering of lines depends on the state of origin mode
known-fail: alacritty – CPR reports the absolute row while origin mode is set
size 10 4
feed \e[2;3r\e[?6h\e[2;4H\e[6n
reply \e[2;4R
cursor 3,4

test DSR 6 reports the last column when a wrap is pending
convention the cursor of a delayed wrap is in the last column
size 10 4
feed abcdefghij\e[6n
reply \e[1;10R

test replies come in the order they were asked
spec xterm ctlseqs: one report for each request
size 10 4
feed \e[5n\e[6n\e[5n
reply \e[0n\e[1;1R\e[0n

test reports are not repeated
convention a reply is sent once, when it is asked for
size 10 4
feed \e[5n
reply \e[0n
reply none

test DECXCPR reports the cursor position
spec xterm ctlseqs CSI ? 6 n: the response is CSI ? r ; c R (VT510 DECXCPR adds the page)
known-fail: alacritty – CSI ? 6 n gets no reply
size 10 4
feed \e[2;3H\e[?6n
reply~ \e[?2;3*R

test DSR ? 15 reports the printer status
spec xterm ctlseqs CSI ? 1 5 n: CSI ? 1 0 n ready, CSI ? 1 1 n not ready (VT510: CSI ? 1 3 n no printer)
known-fail: alacritty – CSI ? Ps n gets no reply
size 10 4
feed \e[?15n
reply~ \e[?1*n

test DSR ? 25 reports the user defined key status
spec xterm ctlseqs CSI ? 2 5 n: CSI ? 2 0 n unlocked, CSI ? 2 1 n locked
known-fail: alacritty – CSI ? Ps n gets no reply
size 10 4
feed \e[?25n
reply~ \e[?2*n

test a DSR that nothing knows gets no answer
convention unknown requests are ignored, not answered with garbage
size 10 4
feed \e[99n
reply none

test enquiry sends the answerback, which is empty by default
spec VT510 Table 4-1 ENQ: sends the answerback message (empty unless set up)
size 10 4
feed \x05
reply none

test primary device attributes
spec xterm ctlseqs CSI Ps c: the response is CSI ? and the terminal's attribute parameters, ending in c
size 10 4
feed \e[c
reply~ \e[?*c

test primary device attributes with an explicit 0
spec xterm ctlseqs CSI Ps c: Ps = 0 or omitted requests the attributes
size 10 4
feed \e[0c
reply~ \e[?*c

test DECID answers like primary device attributes
spec xterm ctlseqs ESC Z: return terminal ID (DECID), the same response as CSI c
size 10 4
feed \eZ
reply~ \e[?*c

test secondary device attributes
spec xterm ctlseqs CSI > Ps c: the response is CSI > Pp ; Pv ; Pc c, Pc is always zero
known-fail: alacritty – the reply is CSI > 0 ; 2600 ; 1 c: Pc is 1, not 0
size 10 4
feed \e[>c
reply~ \e[>*;*;0c

test secondary device attributes with an explicit 0
spec xterm ctlseqs CSI > Ps c: Ps = 0 or omitted
known-fail: alacritty – the reply is CSI > 0 ; 2600 ; 1 c: Pc is 1, not 0
size 10 4
feed \e[>0c
reply~ \e[>*;*;0c

test tertiary device attributes report a unit id
spec VT510 DA3 and DECRPTUI: the response is DCS ! | D...D ST; xterm ctlseqs CSI = Ps c
known-fail: alacritty – CSI = c gets no reply
size 10 4
feed \e[=c
reply~ \eP!|*\e\\

test XTVERSION reports the terminal's name
